'en_content'=><<<'HTML'
<p>Dry-cured ham develops powerful aromas over many months or years of maturation. Not every strong smell is therefore a defect, but a distinctly <strong>sharp, chemical or ammonia-like odour</strong> deserves careful assessment. There is no safe rule that says “if it smells strong, just air it”. You need to consider where the smell comes from, whether it persists, what the surface and cut look like and how the ham has been stored.</p>
<p>A whole leg can combine normal curing, fat, cellar and rind aromas with stronger surface notes. But if an abnormal smell comes from deep muscle, repeats across different areas or appears with slime, decay, wet cavities or unusual colours, the situation is different.</p>
<h2>Where can an ammonia-like smell come from?</h2>
<p>During curing, proteins and fats undergo extensive transformations that create many aroma compounds. The surface also carries microorganisms associated with maturation. Some breakdown of nitrogen-containing compounds can generate sharp alkaline or ammonia-like notes.</p>
<p>A mild surface note is not equivalent to a penetrating odour released from the muscle every time you slice. Intensity, persistence and location are useful clues.</p>
<h2>First determine whether it is outside or inside</h2>
<p>Before carving deeply, inspect the surface. Whole hams can carry cellar moulds and concentrated aromas in rind, external fat and protected areas. Once the area is cleaned correctly and the first oxidised fat is removed, the usable cut may smell very different.</p>
<p>If every new slice or a deeper section still has a strong chemical smell, do not automatically blame the rind.</p>
<h2>Can airing make the smell disappear?</h2>
<p>Some volatile aromas can concentrate during transport or packaging, so allowing a freshly exposed surface to breathe for a few minutes may reduce them. This is also familiar with some vacuum-packed foods.</p>
<p>But <strong>airing does not make spoiled food safe</strong>. If the smell remains strong, increases, returns deeper in the ham or comes with other signs of deterioration, stop eating it.</p>
<h2>Other signs to inspect</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Texture:</strong> an unusually slimy, sticky or wet cut surface.</li>
<li><strong>Colour:</strong> abnormal greenish or grey wet areas rather than normal differences between anatomical sections.</li>
<li><strong>Cavities:</strong> internal pockets with unpleasant odour or liquid.</li>
<li><strong>Fat:</strong> strongly oxidised fat can smell rancid, although rancidity is different from ammonia.</li>
<li><strong>Persistence:</strong> an odour present through several layers is more concerning than a surface note that disappears after cleaning.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Ammonia-like is not the same as rancid</h2>
<p><strong>Rancidity</strong> mainly comes from fat oxidation and can suggest stale nuts, cardboard, paint or old fat. Ammonia-like notes are sharper and more alkaline. Several aroma defects can coexist, so they are not always easy to separate without experience.</p>
<p>If the problem is more clearly fatty or rancid, see our guide to <a href="/en/ham-smells-rancid-why-it-happens-when-to-discard/">rancid smells in cured ham</a>.</p>
<h2>What is the ham “cala”?</h2>
<p>Professional ham makers use a thin tool called a <strong>cala</strong>, inserted into specific points to evaluate internal aroma without cutting the whole leg open. An experienced assessor can detect normal maturation and possible defects in sensitive areas.</p>
<p>This is why a deep aroma problem is not always visible from outside. Our guide to <a href="/en/what-is-ham-cala-how-a-piece-is-checked-without-cutting-it/">ham cala</a> explains the method in more detail.</p>
<h2>Does a strong smell mean the ham is spoiled?</h2>
<p>Not necessarily. Iberian ham can be much more aromatic than cooked ham or mild deli meat, and different people describe cured-fat aromas differently.</p>
<p>The concern is not simply “it smells strong”, but <strong>a persistent unpleasant chemical or putrid smell that does not fit the product and is accompanied by other abnormalities</strong>.</p>
<h2>What to do if you have just opened a ham</h2>
<ol>
<li>Do not keep carving large amounts.</li>
<li>Check whether the smell is only superficial or present on a clean cut.</li>
<li>Remove only enough rind and outer fat to expose the eating area.</li>
<li>Let the cut breathe briefly.</li>
<li>If the abnormal smell persists, photograph it and contact the seller or producer.</li>
<li>Keep the label, batch number and purchase details until the issue is resolved.</li>
</ol>
<p>For an expensive whole ham, documenting the condition before excessive trimming helps the producer assess a possible quality issue.</p>
<h2>Can you simply cut away the bad-smelling area?</h2>
<p>That depends on the cause and cannot be decided from smell alone. A purely superficial issue may leave the interior unaffected. If the odour comes from a deep cavity or repeatedly appears within the muscle, continuing to carve until it “goes away” is not an appropriate strategy.</p>
<p>When there is genuine doubt about a ham's safety or quality, stop consumption and ask the producer to assess it.</p>
<h2>Storage at home</h2>
<p>Before starting a whole ham, keep it in a cool, dry place away from heat according to the producer's directions. Once started, protect the cut without trapping excess moisture and remove only the rind needed for the slices you will carve. See our guide to <a href="/en/how-to-store-iberian-ham-once-started/">storing an Iberian ham once started</a>.</p>
<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>An ammonia-like smell <strong>cannot be diagnosed from one sniff alone</strong>. A surface or temporary note may occur, but a strong persistent smell coming from inside—especially with moisture, slime, cavities or other spoilage odours—should prompt you to stop eating the ham until the producer evaluates it.</p>
